.TH WSYS 4
.SH NAME
wsys \- window system, prototype (processor backend), rio(4)'s files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wsys
[
.B -s
.I srvname
]
[
.B -i
.I cmd
]
[
.B -x
]
.PP
.B mount $wsys /mnt/wsys
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Wsys
is the window system of the
.B ui
branch, a successor of
.IR rio (1)
in the making.
It draws on the screen
.IR draw (3)
gives it (or in the window of
.BR /dev/winname ),
posts a 9P connection as
.BI /srv/ srvname
(default
.BI wsys.$user. pid\fR,
as
.IR rio (1)
does: one that died leaves its entry behind)
and serves a tree of windows.
With
.BR -i ,
it runs
.I cmd
with
.IR rc (1)
in a name space of its own with the tree on
.B /mnt/wsys
and
.B $wsys
naming the service;
.B -x
makes it exit when
.I cmd
does.
.PP
A window's pixels are not sent through the files: they are in global
segments of
.IR segment (3)
that the client makes and owns, its buffers.
As
.I wsys
attaches a segment at the address it has everywhere, each window gets a
range of addresses of its own
.RI ( base ,
.IR size );
the client puts its buffers there, and they cannot collide in
.IR wsys .
A buffer's layout is
.B x8r8g8b8
(32 bits a pixel, little-endian: blue, green, red, unused),
a line every
.I stride
bytes.
Once announced, the client may remove the segment's entry in
.BR #g :
the segment lives while either side has it attached.
.PP
Each window is also a window of
.IR draw (3)
on
.IR wsys 's
screen, border and all, publicly named; and
.I wsys
serves
.IR rio (4)'s
files, so that
.IR draw (3)
programs,
.IR window (1)
and
.I rio
itself run in its windows unchanged.
The attach name
.B new
with
.IR rio 's
options
.RB ( -r ,
.BR -dx ,
.BR -dy ,
.BR -pid ,
.BR -cd )
makes a window and attaches to its directory; a window's number attaches to
an existing one;
.B none
or none at all gives the tree below.
.B wctl
at the root takes
.BR new ;
a window's
.BR wctl ,
.IR rio 's
messages
.RB ( resize ,
.BR move ,
.BR top ,
.BR bottom ,
.BR current ,
.BR delete ,
.BR set ,
.BR new ).
A process
.I wsys
starts in a new window gets a name space with the window's files on
.B /dev
and
.BR /mnt/wsys ,
and a note group of its own;
.B delete
sends it
.BR hangup ,
Del in a window that is not raw
.BR interrupt .
A window lives while its files are open (or the
.B clone
file that made it).
.PP
.TP
.B clone
Opening it makes a window; the open file is then the window's
.BR ctl ,
and the window lives until it is closed.
Ranges of closed windows are given again.
.TP
.B screen
gives
.I x0 y0 x1 y1:
where
.I wsys
draws.
.TP
.IB n /cons ", " consctl ", " cursor ", " kbd ", " label ", " mouse ", " snarf ", " text ", " wctl ", " wdir ", " winid ", " winname
as in
.IR rio (4):
the mouse while it is over the window or a button is held (first an
.B r
message after a change of the window), keys as
.IR kbd (3)
messages to
.B kbd
while it is open, else their runes to
.B cons
(a line at a time unless raw: backspace, ^U, ^D, Del);
.B wctl
reads as
.IR rio 's,
a second read waits for a change; writes to
.B cons
collect in
.BR text .
.TP
.IB n /ctl
Reading gives one line of fields:
.I n
.I x0 y0 x1 y1
(the window's content on the screen)
.I w h
.I base size
(the range for its buffers)
.I focus
(1 if it has the focus).
Messages written:
.RS
.TP
.BI rect " x0 y0 x1 y1
moves the window; with a new size it keeps showing its picture (white
beyond it) until a buffer is presented.
.TP
.BI buffer " b seg w h stride
announces buffer
.I b
(0 to 7): segment
.I seg
of
.I w
by
.I h
pixels.
.I Wsys
attaches it; it must lie in the window's range.
To reuse an address, unbuffer first.
.TP
.BI unbuffer " b
.I wsys
detaches buffer
.IR b .
.TP
.BR present " IbP [Ix0 y0 x1 y1P]"
the pixels of the rectangle (in the buffer's coordinates, which are the
window's; all of the buffer that fits without one) go to the screen.
When
.I wsys
is done with the buffer it sends the event
.BI b " bR:"
the client may write it again.
With two or more buffers the client draws the next picture meanwhile.
.TP
.B top
raises the window and gives it the focus.
.TP
.BI label " text
.TP
.B delete
.RE
.TP
.IB n /event
Reading blocks until there are events; each is a line:
.BI m " x y buttons msec
(the mouse, in the window's coordinates, while it is over the window or a button is held),
.BI k " rune
(a key, the rune in hexadecimal),
.BI r " w h
(a new size),
.BI f " focus
(1: the window got the focus, 0: lost it),
.BI b " b
(buffer
.I b
released).
.PP
.I Wsys
composes on the processor through
.IR draw (3):
a background, the windows bottom to top, each with a border (blue with the focus).
Mouse events come from
.B /dev/mouse
(opened once: the kernel's takes one opener),
keys from
.B /dev/kbd
(else
.B /dev/cons
in raw mode);
a button pressed over a window gives it the focus and raises it;
on its border, button 1 moves the window and button 3 sweeps it to a new
size (the edges or the corner grabbed; an outline until the button comes
up, then the client gets an
.B r
event).
Keys go to the focused window.
.SH EXAMPLES
A client in C: see
.BR /tests/ui/wsystest.c .
.SH SOURCE
.B /sys/src/cmd/wsys
(Rust;
.BR scripts/build/cargo.sh )
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR rio (1),
.IR rio (4),
.IR window (1),
.IR draw (3),
.IR segment (3)
.SH BUGS
A prototype: no labels shown, no text windows of its own (a shell needs
.I rio
in a window), no hidden windows, no menus, no Vulkan backend yet;
.B /dev/screen
and
.B window
images are not served.
.I Wsys
must be allowed to attach the clients' segments: the same user, or the
host owner.
With the processor backend a buffer is released as soon as it is
copied.
When
.I wsys
exits the windows are gone; clients cannot announce theirs again yet.
Each buffer is a segment of
.IR wsys 's
own as well; a process has room for some 250
(of which Rust's threads take 64), so all windows together get about 190 buffers.
.I Rio
in a window breaks when the window goes (its procs, on the note group's
.BR hangup ,
as in a window of
.IR rio ).
